Core Database Concepts

Design a clean relational schema for at least:

users/profiles

portfolios

cash balances

holdings

trades/orders

transactions

stock/asset information

price history

game sessions/seasons

leaderboard data

Do NOT blindly create tables. Think through relationships, indexes, constraints, and data integrity first.

Trading Rules

The backend must NEVER trust the frontend.

For a BUY:

Verify the authenticated user.

Verify the requested asset exists.

Verify the quantity is valid.

Obtain the current price from the server-side source.

Calculate the total transaction cost.

Verify the user has enough virtual cash.

Execute the transaction atomically.

Update the user's cash.

Update/create the holding.

Record the trade.

For a SELL:

Verify the user owns enough shares.

Calculate the transaction value server-side.

Update the holding.

Update cash.

Record the trade.

Prevent:

Negative balances

Negative share quantities

Selling shares the user does not own

Double-spending

Client-side price manipulation

Duplicate transaction execution

Unauthorized access to another user's portfolio

Security

Use Supabase Row Level Security wherever appropriate.

Users should only be able to access data they are authorized to access.

Never expose:

service-role keys

privileged database credentials

secrets

private server-side functionality

Anything involving money, holdings, trades, or game results must be validated server-side.

API / Backend Design

Create clear interfaces that the frontend developer can consume.

Document:

endpoint/function name

request format

response format

errors

authentication requirements

Keep the API predictable and easy to use.

Stock Prices

Design the system so the price provider can be swapped later.

Do not tightly couple the entire application to one market-data provider.

Create a clean abstraction such as:

StockPriceProvider
→ getCurrentPrice(symbol)
→ getHistoricalPrices(symbol)
→ getMarketStatus()

If we initially use simulated prices instead of real-time market data, make that easy to replace later.

Game System

Support the concept of a game/season where appropriate.

A game could have:

starting virtual cash

start date

end date

participating players

portfolio value

leaderboard position

Keep the architecture flexible enough that we can later add:

